#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443 :topic/difficulty 1.2233896690425385 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428224 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1361->30. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "f580d418-6860-5b04-b964-2999aa08b42f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105573 :question/id #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" :question/math-academy-id "e-374" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105574 :question/id #uuid "9c57c69e-efa2-5e82-992c-53570e61af09" :question/math-academy-id "e-375" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\dfrac 1 {2x} + 3x$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1361 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754}] :topic/title "Integrating the Reciprocal Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628 :topic/difficulty 0.8280730284227736 :topic/id #uuid "5a0d0fb8-c0a3-55dc-a497-9f0d5f0eda76" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064203 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064071 :question/id #uuid "eff323d1-4e70-5490-8e41-9fbf00c387f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-446" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int (3x+1)^3 \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(3x+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 4(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(3x+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2b47545-fdf1-5e6a-b0bb-bbbecb8067cc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064204 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064072 :question/id #uuid "e469c042-9619-55d4-b745-d4ed26251d80" :question/math-academy-id "e-447" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2}{(1-x)^3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2(1-x)^{-3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =  -\\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2(1-x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{u}^{-3}\\cdot (-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {u}^{-3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{-2}}{-2}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =(1-x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-2(1-x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064075 :question/id #uuid "e416cb5c-22df-55e4-a3cb-c6b5dfc650c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-448" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int\\sqrt{1-5x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :topic/math-academy-id 334 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101}] :topic/title "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}]}
